WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.475
What the providers keep, as published today.

2
00:00:02.755 --> 00:00:06.829
Your provider is one of the systems that holds copies of your prompts.

3
00:00:07.109 --> 00:00:12.443
These summaries come from each provider’s published policy pages, read on the review date.

4
00:00:12.723 --> 00:00:21.128
Policies change and contracts override defaults, so confirm against the current page and your own agreement before relying on any of it.

5
00:00:21.408 --> 00:00:23.691
Anthropic (Claude API, commercial).

6
00:00:23.971 --> 00:00:41.187
Its privacy center says API inputs and outputs are deleted within 30 days of receipt or generation by default, with exceptions: content flagged by trust and safety systems may be kept up to 2 years, data tied to feedback you submit is kept for 5 years,

7
00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:46.224
and services you control (such as files you upload) follow their own lifetimes.

8
00:00:46.504 --> 00:00:52.349
Its retention docs say retained data is never used for model training without your express permission.

9
00:00:52.629 --> 00:01:05.003
Zero data retention (ZDR) is an arrangement you request through Anthropic’s sales team; it is enabled per organization, and it covers only features marked eligible in a published table.

10
00:01:05.283 --> 00:01:22.051
Stateful features (for example the Files API, code execution containers, and the MCP connector) are not ZDR-eligible, and some models, which Anthropic calls Covered Models, require 30-day retention and are not available under ZDR at all.

11
00:01:22.331 --> 00:01:28.667
Even under ZDR, Anthropic may retain flagged content where required by law or for safety.

12
00:01:28.947 --> 00:01:38.419
For health data there is a separate HIPAA readiness arrangement with a signed business associate agreement (BAA), which blocks non-eligible features with an error.

13
00:01:38.699 --> 00:01:40.448
OpenAI (API).

14
00:01:40.728 --> 00:01:46.488
Its data controls guide says API data is not used to train models unless you opt in.

15
00:01:46.768 --> 00:01:50.608
Abuse-monitoring logs are kept for up to 30 days by default.

16
00:01:50.888 --> 00:01:57.075
Zero Data Retention and Modified Abuse Monitoring both require OpenAI’s approval.

17
00:01:57.355 --> 00:02:14.741
Application state is a separate question: the Responses API stores response data for at least 30 days by default (or when store is true), and several endpoints, including files, assistants, threads, vector stores, batches, and fine-tuning, are not ZDR-eligible.

18
00:02:15.021 --> 00:02:17.624
Google (Gemini API and Vertex AI).

19
00:02:17.904 --> 00:02:35.931
The Gemini API terms distinguish Unpaid Services (free AI Studio use and unpaid API quota), where Google uses submitted content and responses to improve its products and human reviewers may read them, and the terms tell you not to submit sensitive, confidential,

20
00:02:36.211 --> 00:02:48.243
or personal information; from Paid Services, where Google doesn’t use prompts or responses to improve products but logs them for a limited period for abuse detection and legal requirements.

21
00:02:48.523 --> 00:02:53.984
Apps serving users in the EEA, Switzerland, or the UK must use Paid Services.

22
00:02:54.264 --> 00:03:11.800
On Vertex AI, Google’s docs say it won’t train on your data without permission, but zero retention takes configuration: in-memory caching is on by default (data deleted within 24 hours) and can be disabled, request-response logging must stay off, and some features,

23
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.965
such as Grounding with Google Search, store data for days in a way you can’t turn off.

24
00:03:17.245 --> 00:03:32.627
The pattern across all three: no training on paid API traffic by default, a default retention window for abuse monitoring, ZDR as an approved arrangement rather than a switch, and feature-level exceptions that break ZDR quietly.

25
00:03:32.907 --> 00:03:35.957
When a privacy review asks “does our provider keep this?

26
00:03:36.237 --> 00:03:40.995
”, the right answer names the arrangement, the features in use, and the date you checked.
